  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 429 AM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nIR imagery early this morning shows an approaching trough closing in \non Wyoming, with a cloud shield now extending from southern Nevada \nthrough Idaho. This will bring a welcome return of moisture to the \narea, starting first across western Wyoming. Rain and mountain snow \nwill move in late this morning, spreading northeast through the day. \nThis will mostly be limited to areas along and west of the \nContinental Divide through sunset. Locations east of the Divide will \nsee one more warm day, with temperatures peaking 10 to near 20 \ndegrees above normal. But for most of these areas, this will also \ncome with a wind tax. The entire Wind Corridor, from Sweetwater\nCounty through southern Johnson County, can expect \nsouthwesterly gusts of 30 to 45 mph this afternoon. This will \ncome ahead of a cold front that will push into western Wyoming \naround sunset. This will lower snow levels across the west down \nto valley bottoms tonight. Most of the snow will remain in the \nmountains, however, particularly across the Tetons, southwestern\nYellowstone National Park, and the western slopes of the Wind \nRiver Range. Accumulations here are now in the 5 to 10 inch \nrange above 9,000 feet. Winter Weather Advisories are now in \neffect for portions of these areas, most notably covering Teton \nand Togwotee passes which could see 3 to 6 inches. Snowfall \nrates will be heaviest ahead of the front this afternoon as \nupper level lift is maximized, as well as behind the front \ntonight as upslope flow increases. With this afternoon's \nprecipitation falling as rain across the western valleys, \naccumulating snow will be limited, with only 1 to 2 inches \nexpected by Wednesday morning.\n\nMeanwhile, wind will ramp up further late this evening along \nand east of the Divide as a surface low develops across northern\nWyoming ahead of the advancing cold front. This will lead to \ngusts of 55 to 65 mph across the more susceptible areas of the \nWind Corridor, though gusts of 35 to 45 mph will be more \nwidespread elsewhere. High Wind Warnings are now in effect for \nthe strongest wind gusts, most notably in the Casper area.\n\nPrecipitation chances increase east of the Divide tonight, and \nwith the cold front some locations may even find a light dusting\nof snow Wednesday morning. Overall, moisture across the basins \nis expected to be low, generally under a tenth of an inch. The \nBighorns will fare better, with accumulating snowfall expected \ntonight through Wednesday morning. Forecast snow totals across \nthe higher elevations here have been increasing as of late (4 to\n8 inches). This bears watching for a possible additional Winter\nWeather Advisory; confidence on totals was not high enough to \nissue one this morning. \n\nOne other area to watch will be the I-80 Corridor in Sweetwater\nCounty after sunset tonight. There will be enough instability \nfor showers and even a few thunderstorms this afternoon and \nevening, with most of the associated precipitation falling as \nrain. However, temperatures will drop with the front, which \ncould lead to some snow squall activity late this evening and \nearly Wednesday morning. Models have been picking up on the \npotential for light snow up to an inch with this activity. This \ncould lead to some travel impacts tonight along I-80, especially\nwhen combined with the gusty winds. \n\nPrecipitation will come to an end everywhere by late Wednesday \nmorning. A cooler airmass will keep temperatures much more \nseasonable with highs behind the system in the 30s and 40s. The \nnext system will follow quickly, with most models bringing it \ninto western Wyoming Thursday morning and spreading \nprecipitation chances across remaining areas. Forecast \nprecipitation totals have not changed much, though perhaps \ntrended slightly down as of late, with most valleys and basins \nforecast to see less than a tenth of an inch. Regardless, cooler\nand more unsettled conditions are expected to continue through \nthis weekend.",
